  "description": "Recorded Viking Age names like Astrid, Sigrid, Bjorn, Leif and Thorfinn, with the Old Norse spelling and what each part of the name meant.",
  "section": "heritage",
  "published": "2026-11-13",
  "motif": "longship",
  "intro": "<p>These are names recorded for people in the Viking Age and the sagas, given in the spelling most people use today with the Old Norse form in the note: Astrid is Ástríðr, Bjorn is Bjǫrn. Some, like Astrid, Ingrid and Leif, are still in use; others, like Thorfinn and Gunnhild, are mainly found in history and stories.</p><p>Like Old English names, most were built from two words, so we give the meaning of each part. We checked them against Behind the Name, the Cleasby–Vigfusson Icelandic-English Dictionary and the Society for Name Studies in Britain and Ireland.</p>",
