  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Thor, bears, wolves and battles",
      "content": "<p>A handful of building blocks turn up again and again. The god Thor (<em>Þórr</em>) starts dozens of names: Thorstein, Thorkel, Thorfinn, Thora, Thordis. <em>Áss</em>, \"god\", gives Astrid, Asbjorn and Asgeir. Animals are common too: <em>bjǫrn</em> (bear), <em>úlfr</em> (wolf, written <em>-ólfr</em> at the end of a name, as in Ingolf) and <em>hrafn</em> (raven).</p><p>Women's names often end in <em>-hildr</em>, a poetic word for battle (Gunnhild, Ragnhild), <em>-dís</em>, goddess (Thordis, Vigdis), or <em>-rún</em>, secret lore (Gudrun, Sigrun). As with Old English names, the parts were chosen by family custom, so \"Thor's bear\" is a list of parts rather than a description.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Spelling Old Norse names",
      "content": "<p>Old Norse used letters English no longer has. <strong>Þ</strong> (thorn) and <strong>ð</strong> (eth) are both <em>th</em>, and <strong>ǫ</strong> is an open <em>o</em>, often written <em>ö</em> or <em>o</em> today. Most men's names end in <em>-r</em> in Old Norse (Leifr, Haraldr), which is usually dropped in English: Leif, Harald.</p><p>Modern Scandinavian spellings differ again: Bjǫrn is Björn in Swedish and Bjørn in Norwegian and Danish, and Þorsteinn is Torsten. For a baby, the simple English spelling is easiest. For a story set in the Viking Age, the Old Norse forms in the notes look more authentic.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Names the Vikings left in England",
      "content": "<p>Scandinavian settlers brought these names to England in the 800s and 900s, and some are still on the map. Orm is in the Ormesbys of Norfolk, Lincolnshire and Yorkshire, Áslákr is in Aslackby, Halfdan is in Holdenby, and a woman called Sigrid is remembered in Sizergh in Westmorland. A few became English surnames, such as Herrick from Eiríkr, and the Norse Haraldr reinforced the Old English Hereweald, which is why Harold was so common in England.</p>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What is the most common Viking name?",
      "answer": "There are no reliable counts for the Viking Age. The god Thor (Þórr) is the best-known name element, and names built on it, on Ás- (god), -bjǫrn (bear) and -úlfr (wolf) turn up again and again. Thorstein, Thorkel, Bjorn, Ulf and Ketil are typical men's names, and Thora, Gunnhild and Ragnhild typical women's."
    },
    {
      "question": "Which Old Norse names mean wolf?",
      "answer": "Ulf (Úlfr) means wolf on its own. The element also appears in Ulfhild (\"wolf + battle\") and, written -ólfr, at the end of names such as Ingolf. Hrolf comes from Hróðulfr, \"fame + wolf\"."
    },
    {
      "question": "How did Old Norse names work?",
      "answer": "A person had one given name, often made of two parts such as Þór + steinn, plus a patronymic from their father's name, such as Sveinsson or Sveinsdóttir. Many people also had a byname describing them, such as hárfagri, \"fair-haired\"."
    }
  ],
